WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  back now at 7 30 on cbs mornings as  russia increases its attacks on  ukrainian cities there is a growing  international push to charge russia's  leaders with war crimes the list of  alleged violations includes the bombing  of a maternity hospital the shelling of  residential areas and an attack on a  nuclear power plant  russian president vladimir putin is  among those who potentially could be  charged senior investigative  correspondent catherine harris reports  the evidence could come together rapidly  however getting a conviction would be  hard indeed catherine good morning  good morning tony our investigative team  found the russian aggression in ukraine  is so severe and events unfolding so  rapidly that a war crimes case could  come together quickly as a warning the  video in this report is graphic and may  be disturbing to some people  beyond the indiscriminate shelling of  civilian targets and the mortar attack  on fleeing families vladimir putin  crossed a perilous threshold when a  russian assault set off a fire at  europe's largest nuclear reactor this is  the most direct case really since since  world war ii of having a certainly a  great power  engaging in this kind of attack on the  civilian population stephen rapp is a  former u.s ambassador at large for war  crimes who's built cases against  dictators and sought accountability for  genocide how will investigators build a  war crimes case against the russian  president the first way that they can  build it and it may be sufficient in  this case  they can look at the pattern of the  attack  if there is a war crimes designation  against vladimir putin what would it  mean well it would mean that he's an  international pariah it also means that  he can't travel anywhere  no more summits  because he'd be putting himself in  danger the u.s embassy in kyiv called  the attack on the nuclear plant a war  crime but hours later the white house  dialed back the language we have not  made conclusions it's a legal review and  a process that goes through the  administration so the bar is very high  it's very high but just given the  intensity of this campaign and the  civilian destruction i think that can be  developed in a matter of weeks the  international criminal court's chief  prosecutor karim khan has opened an  investigation into russia's conduct in  ukraine the u.s is not part of the court  which has a track record of 10  convictions over two decades securing  justice there does not happen quickly  the court had to pull the arrest warrant  for libyan leader muammar gaddafi after  he was executed by his own people and  syria's president bashar al-assad  remains in power nearly a decade after  he allegedly used chemical weapons  against the syrian people is there any  past indication that vladimir putin  cares about being branded a war criminal  i don't think so matthew cranig worked  the russia and nuclear portfolios under  the bush obama and trump administrations  he told cbs news there's a rising threat  from russia's stockpile of lower yield  weapons known as tactical nukes these  are weapons that would be used for  winning a battle  on the field in ukraine is the use of a  tactical nuclear weapon against  civilians a war crime yes a war crime of  the highest order this week intelligence  officials warned of a russian policy  called escalate to de-escalate we  obviously take it very seriously that  allows for the use of smaller tactical  nuclear weapons on the battlefield to  intimidate an adversary i think before  he lost the war he would try to use  nuclear weapons to try to pull victory  from the jaws of defeat some people  listening to that may say that you're  being an alarmist  are you being an alarmist well i think  the risk of nuclear war is still low but  i think the risk is higher now than any  time we've seen in in recent years  when you hear those sirens where does  your heart take you well it always takes  me uh to the side of those people that  are there and to families whose children  are dying and obviously it always  motivates me to say what can we do to to  bring that guy in yesterday at the state  department briefing a spokesman said  they've seen credible reporting of  deliberate attacks on civilians which  would under the geneva conventions  constitute a war crime though not part  of the international criminal court the  spokesman said the u.s is gathering  evidence for a possible prosecution dana  all right catherine and as you reminded  us in the piece the difference between  that charge and then actual  accountability or punishment thank you

